\title{The Periodic Table as a Spectrum:\\
Elements as Eigenvalues of a Self-Adjoint Operator}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The periodic table orders the chemical elements by atomic number $Z$, the integer
charge of the nucleus. We recast this ordering spectrally. On the
$n$-dimensional real Hilbert space of coordinate lists we define a single
self-adjoint operator---the \emph{nuclear Hamiltonian} $H_n$---as the diagonal
operator whose entries are the atomic numbers $1, 2, \dots, n$. We prove that
$H_n$ is a genuine quantum observable (self-adjoint) and that its spectrum is
\emph{exactly} the set of atomic numbers, with both inclusions established: every
atomic number is an eigenvalue, and every eigenvalue is an atomic number. We then
develop the operator's spectral invariants and show that each one recovers a
classical arithmetic identity: the trace equals the Gauss triangular number
$n(n+1)/2$; the determinant equals $n!$; the characteristic polynomial factors
into linear terms rooted at the atomic numbers; and, most generally, the trace of
the $k$-th power of $H_n$ equals the $k$-th power sum $\sum_i z_i^{\,k}$, a full
power-sum ladder of which the trace identity is the first rung. The result is a
clean cross-domain bridge between spectral/linear algebra and elementary number
theory, packaged around a physically motivated reinterpretation of the periodic
table. We conclude with several natural extensions: off-diagonal (Jacobi)
couplings and interlacing, infinite-dimensional and unbounded formulations,
isotope-weighted multiplicities, and spectral zeta/heat-trace invariants.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

The periodic table is organized by the atomic number $Z$: the count of protons in
a nucleus. Since Moseley's X-ray experiments in 1913, $Z$ has been understood as
the true ordinal of an element, superseding atomic weight. Yet $Z$ is, in the
end, a \emph{label}---an integer index attached to a physical species.

The purpose of this paper is to argue, and to prove, that the atomic numbers are
better understood not as primitive labels but as the \textbf{spectrum} of a
single operator. In quantum mechanics an observable is a self-adjoint operator,
and the values it can return upon measurement are exactly its eigenvalues. We
show that there is a natural self-adjoint operator whose set of eigenvalues is
precisely the atomic numbers of the first $n$ elements. In this sense the
periodic table \emph{is} a spectrum.

The construction is deliberately minimal: we take the diagonal operator with the
atomic numbers on the diagonal. What is interesting is not the construction but
its consequences. Because the object is an operator, it carries the full battery
of spectral invariants---trace, determinant, characteristic polynomial, and the
higher spectral moments (power-sum traces). We prove that each of these
invariants coincides with a classical object of elementary number theory:
triangular numbers, factorials, and power sums. The periodic table thereby
becomes a dictionary translating spectral quantities into arithmetic ones.

The contribution is threefold:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A \emph{faithful spectral realization} of the periodic table: an explicit
    self-adjoint operator whose spectrum equals the set of atomic numbers, with
    both inclusions rigorously established (Section~\ref{sec:spectrum}).
  \item A \emph{family of spectral--arithmetic bridges} identifying the trace
    with the triangular number, the determinant with the factorial, the
    characteristic polynomial with a product of linear factors, and the $k$-th
    power trace with the $k$-th power sum (Section~\ref{sec:bridges}).
  \item A \emph{program of extensions} turning the reframing into a research
    direction (Section~\ref{sec:future}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions and Setup}

Throughout, $n$ is a fixed nonnegative integer (the number of elements under
consideration), and we work over the real numbers. We index elements by
$i \in \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ and use a one-based atomic number.

\subsection{The state space}
Let $V_n = \mathbb{R}^n$ be the space of real coordinate lists of length $n$,
regarded as a finite-dimensional real Hilbert space with the standard inner
product. The standard basis vectors $e_0, \dots, e_{n-1}$ are the ``single slot''
states.

\subsection{Atomic numbers}
\begin{definition}[Atomic number]
The atomic number of the $i$-th element is the real scalar
\[
  z_i \;=\; i + 1, \qquad i \in \{0, \dots, n-1\},
\]
so that $z_0 = 1, z_1 = 2, \dots, z_{n-1} = n$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The nuclear Hamiltonian}
\begin{definition}[Nuclear Hamiltonian]
The \emph{nuclear Hamiltonian} is the diagonal operator
\[
  H_n \;=\; \diag(z_0, z_1, \dots, z_{n-1}) \;=\; \diag(1, 2, \dots, n)
\]
acting on $V_n$. Explicitly, $(H_n x)_i = z_i\, x_i$ for every state $x \in V_n$
and every index $i$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Eigenvalues and spectrum}
Recall that $\mu \in \mathbb{R}$ is an \emph{eigenvalue} of a linear operator $T$
on $V_n$ if there exists a nonzero vector $x$ with $Tx = \mu x$. The
\emph{spectrum} of $T$ is the set $\sigma(T)$ of its eigenvalues. In finite
dimensions the spectrum coincides with the set of eigenvalues.

\section{The Spectrum Is the Periodic Table}\label{sec:spectrum}

\subsection{Self-adjointness}
\begin{theorem}[Observable]\label{thm:hermitian}
The nuclear Hamiltonian $H_n$ is self-adjoint: $H_n^{*} = H_n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A diagonal matrix with real diagonal entries equals its own conjugate transpose,
because transposition fixes diagonal entries and the entries are real so
conjugation fixes them too. Hence $H_n^{*} = H_n$.
\end{proof}

Self-adjointness guarantees that all measured values are real and that the
operator has a complete orthonormal eigenbasis---the requirements of a physical
observable.

\subsection{Every atomic number is an eigenvalue}
\begin{theorem}[Elements are spectral lines]\label{thm:eig-forward}
For each index $i$, the atomic number $z_i$ is an eigenvalue of $H_n$, with
eigenstate the standard basis vector $e_i$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Compute $H_n e_i$ coordinatewise: $(H_n e_i)_j = z_j (e_i)_j$, which equals $z_i$
when $j = i$ and $0$ otherwise. Hence $H_n e_i = z_i\, e_i$, and since $e_i \ne 0$,
$z_i$ is an eigenvalue with eigenvector $e_i$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Every eigenvalue is an atomic number}
\begin{theorem}[No ghost eigenvalues]\label{thm:eig-backward}
If $\mu$ is an eigenvalue of $H_n$, then $\mu = z_j$ for some index $j$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $x \ne 0$ satisfy $H_n x = \mu x$. Some coordinate $x_j$ is nonzero. Reading
off coordinate $j$ of the eigenvalue equation gives $z_j\, x_j = \mu\, x_j$;
cancelling $x_j \ne 0$ yields $\mu = z_j$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The spectral realization}
\begin{theorem}[The periodic table is a spectrum]\label{thm:spectrum}
The spectrum of the nuclear Hamiltonian equals the set of atomic numbers:
\[
  \sigma(H_n) \;=\; \{\, z_i : 0 \le i < n \,\}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The inclusion $\supseteq$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:eig-forward}; the inclusion
$\subseteq$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:eig-backward}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The eigenvalues are the integers $1, \dots, n$]
\[
  \{\, z_i : 0 \le i < n \,\} \;=\; \{\, k \in \mathbb{Z} : 1 \le k \le n \,\}.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
As $i$ ranges over $\{0, \dots, n-1\}$, $z_i = i+1$ ranges over $\{1, \dots, n\}$
bijectively; conversely any integer $k$ with $1 \le k \le n$ equals $z_{k-1}$.
\end{proof}

\section{Spectral--Arithmetic Bridges}\label{sec:bridges}

\subsection{Trace and the triangular number}
\begin{theorem}[Trace--triangular bridge]\label{thm:trace}
\[
  \tr(H_n) \;=\; \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} z_i \;=\; 1 + 2 + \cdots + n \;=\;
  \frac{n(n+1)}{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The trace of a diagonal matrix is the sum of its diagonal entries, $\sum_i z_i =
\sum_{k=1}^n k$. Induction on $n$: the base case $n=0$ gives $0$, and the
inductive step adds $(k+1)$ to $k(k+1)/2$ to give $(k+1)(k+2)/2$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Determinant and the factorial}
\begin{theorem}[Determinant--factorial bridge]\label{thm:det}
\[
  \det(H_n) \;=\; \prod_{i=0}^{n-1} z_i \;=\; 1 \cdot 2 \cdots n \;=\; n!.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The determinant of a diagonal matrix is the product of its diagonal entries,
$\prod_{k=1}^n k$. Induction: $n=0$ gives the empty product $1 = 0!$, and the
inductive step multiplies $k!$ by $(k+1)$ to give $(k+1)!$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The characteristic polynomial}
\begin{theorem}[Characteristic polynomial factorization]\label{thm:charpoly}
The characteristic polynomial of $H_n$ factors completely into linear terms
rooted at the atomic numbers:
\[
  \chi_{H_n}(X) \;=\; \det(X\,I - H_n) \;=\; \prod_{i=0}^{n-1} \big(X - z_i\big)
  \;=\; \prod_{k=1}^{n} (X - k).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The matrix $X I - H_n$ is diagonal with entries $X - z_i$, and the determinant of
a diagonal matrix is the product of the diagonal. The roots are exactly the
$z_i$, consistent with Theorem~\ref{thm:spectrum}.
\end{proof}

The expanded coefficients of $\chi_{H_n}$ are (up to sign) the elementary
symmetric polynomials of $1, \dots, n$---the unsigned Stirling numbers of the
first kind.

\subsection{The power-sum ladder}
\begin{theorem}[Power-sum ladder]\label{thm:powersum}
For every nonnegative integer $k$,
\[
  \tr\big(H_n^{\,k}\big) \;=\; \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} z_i^{\,k} \;=\;
  1^k + 2^k + \cdots + n^k.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $H_n = \diag(z_i)$ is diagonal, its $k$-th power is $\diag(z_i^{\,k})$, whose
trace is $\sum_i z_i^{\,k}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cll}
\toprule
$k$ & $\tr(H_n^{\,k}) = \sum_{k'=1}^n (k')^{k}$ & closed form \\
\midrule
$0$ & $\sum 1$ & $n$ \\
$1$ & $\sum k'$ & $\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}$ \\
$2$ & $\sum (k')^2$ & $\dfrac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6}$ \\
$3$ & $\sum (k')^3$ & $\left(\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\right)^2$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The moments (power sums) and the coefficients of $\chi_{H_n}$ (elementary
symmetric polynomials) are linked by Newton's identities, closing the loop between
the two families of invariants.

\section{Algorithms}

The results are constructive and immediately computable.

\subsection{Assemble the nuclear Hamiltonian}
Given $n$, produce the $n \times n$ diagonal matrix with entries $1, \dots, n$.
Complexity $O(n^2)$ dense, or $O(n)$ as a diagonal vector.

\subsection{Verify the spectrum}
Compute eigenvalues numerically and compare (up to sorting and tolerance) against
$\{1, \dots, n\}$; for the diagonal matrix the eigenvalues are read off the
diagonal, giving a self-checking oracle.

\subsection{Spectral invariants versus closed forms}
Compare the trace, determinant, and $k$-th power traces against $n(n+1)/2$, $n!$,
and $\sum_{k'=1}^n (k')^k$. Verify the characteristic polynomial's roots are
$1, \dots, n$.

\subsection{Newton's identities check}
From the power sums $p_1, \dots, p_n$ recover the elementary symmetric
polynomials via
\[
  e_k = \frac{1}{k}\sum_{j=1}^{k} (-1)^{j-1} e_{k-j}\, p_j,
\]
and confirm the $e_k$ match the coefficients of $\chi_{H_n}$.

\section{Extensions and Future Directions}\label{sec:future}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Complex / infinite dimensional.} Move from $\mathbb{R}^n$ to a
    separable Hilbert space and treat the operator as a (possibly unbounded)
    self-adjoint operator, recovering the spectrum as a closed subset of
    $\mathbb{R}$.
  \item \textbf{Non-diagonal Hamiltonians.} Add off-diagonal ``coupling'' terms
    (e.g.\ a tridiagonal Jacobi matrix) and study how the spectrum deforms; prove
    interlacing (Cauchy) bounds relating the perturbed spectrum to the atomic
    numbers.
  \item \textbf{Isotope multiplicities.} Weight each eigenvalue by the number of
    stable isotopes, giving a self-adjoint operator on a space whose dimension is
    the total isotope count, and connect the trace to observed isotope
    statistics.
  \item \textbf{Spectral zeta / partition function.} Study $\sum_i z_i^{-s}$ and
    $\sum_i \exp(-\beta z_i)$ (the heat trace), linking to $\zeta$-values and to
    the thermodynamics of the model.
  \item \textbf{Power-sum invariants.} The power-sum ladder is realized by
    Theorem~\ref{thm:powersum}. A natural next step is to connect these power sums
    back to the elementary symmetric functions via Newton's identities, closing
    the loop between the spectral moments and the coefficients of the
    characteristic polynomial.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}

By taking the simplest operator whose spectrum is the atomic numbers---the
diagonal one---we isolate exactly what is intrinsic to the periodic table (its
ordered set of integer labels) from what is a modeling choice (couplings,
dynamics). The periodic table's arithmetic is spectral: Gauss's triangular
number, the factorial, the sum-of-powers formulas, and the elementary symmetric
polynomials are invariants of a single observable whose spectrum is the elements.
The reframing is also generative: each extension above is a well-posed problem
that would not be visible if one read the periodic table as a static list.

\section{Conclusion}

We defined a self-adjoint nuclear Hamiltonian whose spectrum is exactly the atomic
numbers $1, \dots, n$, and showed that its principal spectral invariants recover
classical arithmetic: the trace is the triangular number, the determinant is the
factorial, the characteristic polynomial factors over the atomic numbers, and the
$k$-th power trace is the $k$-th power sum. Read this way, the periodic table is
not a list of integers but the spectrum of an operator---and its familiar
arithmetic is that operator's spectral shadow.

\end{document}

